INT. S.S. MARGUERITE - PURSER'S OFFICE - NIGHT - 1912

Brass lamp. Rolling floor. A stack of passenger manifests slides with every swell.

PURSER HOLLOWAY, 50s, neat as a pin, drags a STOWAWAY by the collar into the office. The Stowaway is small, soot-faced, in a boy's cap and oversized coat. Pip, a young DECKHAND, hovers by the door.

HOLLOWAY
Another one, Captain, in the coal bunker!

CAPTAIN VANE, 60s, white beard, brass buttons, looks up from his chart without hurry.

VANE
A boy?

STOWAWAY
(a woman's voice, defiant)
I am no boy.

The cap falls. Dark curls spill out. Holloway lets go as if burned.

VANE
Name?

STOWAWAY
Clementine Ashworth. And you will take me to New York.

HOLLOWAY
She'll be put off at Queenstown!

VANE
Mr. Holloway, we left Queenstown yesterday.

CLEMENTINE
There! You see?

Pip snickers. Holloway glares at him.

VANE
Miss Ashworth, what is in New York?

CLEMENTINE
(softer)
My father. He thinks I'm dead.

The lamp swings. Everyone watches the shadows swing with it.

VANE
Pip. Fetch Miss Ashworth some tea and a blanket.

PIP
Aye, sir!

He's gone. Holloway sputters.

HOLLOWAY
Captain, regulations!

VANE
Regulations don't swim, Mr. Holloway. And neither, I suspect, do you.

He turns the chart. Holloway slumps into his chair. Clementine laughs, and the ship rolls on.
